# No Inline Imports Rule

## Critical Rules

- All imports MUST be declared at the top of the file before any code
- NEVER use `await import()` inside function bodies unless absolutely necessary
- NEVER use `require()` inside function bodies
- If you need dynamic imports for code splitting, document why in a comment
- The only valid exceptions are:
  - Breaking circular dependencies (must be commented)
  - Dynamic code splitting in frontend (must be commented)
  - Plugin systems that require runtime loading (must be commented)

## Examples

<example>
  // Good - Imports at top
  import { logger } from '../logger.js';
  import { getCurrentPeriod, ALL_TIME_PERIOD } from '../utils/periods.js';
  
  class MyService {
    private someMethod() {
      const period = getCurrentPeriod();
      // Use imports normally
    }
  }
</example>

<example type="invalid">
  // Bad - Inline import inside method
  class MyService {
    private async someMethod() {
      const { getCurrentPeriod } = await import('../utils/periods.js');
      const period = getCurrentPeriod();
    }
  }
  
  // Bad - No justification for dynamic import
  function processData() {
    const utils = require('../utils/helper.js');
    return utils.process();
  }
</example>

<example>
  // Acceptable - Dynamic import with clear reason
  async function loadPlugin(name: string) {
    // Dynamic import required: plugin name determined at runtime
    const plugin = await import(`./plugins/${name}.js`);
    return plugin;
  }
</example>
